From 059cfcc25a7a133fbe841d5e9bf7177c00756c15 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <jpm820@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:20:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] NextBSD: enable unprivileged chroot by default

Ship the NEXTBSD kernel with unprivileged chroot enabled by default: flip
the initial value of unprivileged_chroot (backing the
security.bsd.unprivileged_chroot sysctl) from 0 to 1 in
sys/kern/vfs_syscalls.c.

Needed so a regular (non-root) user can enter the single /compat/linux
root as themselves -- landing in their home and running their AppImage /
portable app -- without `sudo chroot` (nextbsd/nextbsd#529). chroot(1)
already sets no_new_privs with -n; with this default on, a non-root
process that has set no_new_privs may call chroot(2).

Same reasoning as 0008 (vfs.usermount) and 0009 (elf64 fallback_brand):
launchd is PID 1 with no rc.d, so /etc/sysctl.conf is never read, and the
kernel default is the only persistent mechanism.

The sysctl stays plain CTLFLAG_RW (upstream), rather than 0008's RWTUN:
the compile-time default is 1, so it is on from boot with no config; an
admin who wants it off runs `sysctl security.bsd.unprivileged_chroot=0`
at runtime. There is no persistent loader.conf off-switch without RWTUN;
that is accepted here.

Safety: chroot(2) is view-confinement, not permission-confinement -- the
process still needs the DAC rights to touch anything -- and the call is
additionally gated on PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS (vfs_syscalls.c:989), which blocks
privilege escalation via setuid. The intended use is a user jailing
themselves into /compat/linux; the no_new_privs gate is what makes the
global default acceptable.

Refs nextbsd/nextbsd#529. Carried out-of-tree for NextBSD.
---
 sys/kern/vfs_syscalls.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/kern/vfs_syscalls.c b/sys/kern/vfs_syscalls.c
index 0c4eeb584..8f467593c 100644
--- a/sys/kern/vfs_syscalls.c
+++ b/sys/kern/vfs_syscalls.c
@@ -964,7 +964,7 @@ kern_chdir(struct thread *td, const char *path, enum uio_seg pathseg)
 	return (0);
 }
 
-static int unprivileged_chroot = 0;
+static int unprivileged_chroot = 1;
 SYSCTL_INT(_security_bsd, OID_AUTO, unprivileged_chroot, CTLFLAG_RW,
     &unprivileged_chroot, 0,
     "Unprivileged processes can use chroot(2)");
-- 
